			<div class="content" id="post-content-4662352">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Also, why do so many articles use the phrase: 'climate-controlled storage area'?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because many objects are sensitive to humidity and temperature.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But there has to be a greater degree of creativity in containment procedures.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In universe, having everything be in some very specific customized facility doesn't make much sense. If you are the Containment Specialist, what you really want to do is get many ideniitcal standardized units and put any objects that can safely be stored in them. You only want to have to build a custom containment unit when you absolutely <em>must</em>. This way is significantly safer, more secure, and cheaper; it also allows you to put most objects into suitable containment right away.</p>
<p>Out of universe, this way everything in the ConProcs is in service of the story and you aren't wasting the reader's time with unnecessary details about what kind of locks and how high the ceiling is.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also why do articles have the containment procedures first?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In universe, because they are what the document is primarily for. These are Special Containment Procedures files, after all. The most important thing to the Foundation is how to contain the thing.</p>
<p>Out of universe, good use of the ConProcs hooks the reader and leaves them wondering just what the hell would warrant such methods.</p>

							</div>

								
